// The Ceres calling convention.
//
// Nothing in the machine enforces any of this: `call` pushes a return address and `ret` pops it,
// and that is the whole of what the hardware knows about subroutines. What follows is a contract
// between the code you write, kept in one place so it is written the same way every time.
//
// See docs/24-Calling-Convention.md for the reasoning, the register roles and worked examples.
//
//   Arguments   r0-r3, then the caller's outgoing area at [sp + 0], [sp + 4], ...
//   Return      r0 (f0 for a float)
//   Callee-saved  r8-r11, f8-f15, fp
//   Caller-saved  r0-r7, r13, f0-f7, and the flags
//   Never touch   r12 - ldv, stv and the float form of la clobber it
//
// The frame, after `proc_enter`:
//
//   [fp + 8], [fp + 12], ...   arguments five and up, as the caller left them
//   [fp + 4]                   return address, pushed by `call`
//   [fp + 0]                   saved fp, pushed by `enter`
//   [sp + 0] .. [sp + N-4]     this function's own frame: outgoing args first, then
//                              saved registers and locals
//
// `sp` must not move between `proc_enter` and `proc_leave`. Everything a function needs lives in
// the frame it reserved once, which is what keeps every `[sp + Frame.field]` valid for the whole
// body - including across a `call`.

// Opens a frame of $frame_size bytes. Pass a struct's own name and the size comes from it:
//
//   struct Frame
//       outgoing0: u32
//       saved_r8:  u32
//       count:     u32
//   endstruct
//
//   my_function:
//       proc_enter Frame
//       str r8, [sp + Frame.saved_r8]
//       ...
//
// A function that takes four arguments or fewer, keeps everything in r0-r7 and calls nothing needs
// no frame at all - just `ret`.
global macro proc_enter $frame_size
    enter
    sub sp, sp, $frame_size
endmacro

// Closes the frame and returns. `leave` restores sp from fp, so the frame size is not repeated
// here and a function may have as many exits as it likes.
global macro proc_leave
    leave
    ret
endmacro
